# EXPECT: `local` is not an exit here (a function that interrogates a name is traced, and both `local`-shadow guards refuse an observed/temporal function first)
# (#218) The BOXED member of the class: a module string that a function
# both assigns and interrogates. On the VM the write binds function-local
# (prev of z = "a", module z = "a"); the interrogated call-local pair is a C
# double, so this stays a loud by-name refusal until a Value* pair exists.
#
# THE EXPECT LINE PINS THE MESSAGE'S EXIT SENTENCE, not just the refusal
# (#141/#218 residuals review). The message used to say "Mark it `local` to
# make the VM's binding explicit" and that exit was DEAD for every member of
# the class: interrogating a name makes the function traced, and both
# `local`-shadow guards test `(g_observed == 1) or (g_traced == 1)` first, so
# this very program with `local z is "b"` dies
#   AOT: `local z` shadowing a module binding is not supported here
#        (observed/temporal function -- history and env state are keyed by
#        NAME, invisible to a per-call env)
# on the parent commit and on this one. Measured on all five refuse fixtures
# of the class: `local` refused 5/5, renaming the module binding transpiled
# 5/5. A refusal may only name exits that are reachable for the shape it
# refuses; pinning the sentence here is what stops the wording rotting back.
z is "a"
define u() as:
    z is "b"
    return prev of z
print of (u of [])
print of z
